'''Mayi-Kutuna''', also spelt '''Mayaguduna''', '''Maikudunu''' and other variants, is an [[language extinction|extinct]] [[Mayabic languages|Mayabic language]] once spoken by the [[Mayi-Kutuna]], an [[Aboriginal Australian]] people of the present-day [[Cape York Peninsula]] in northern [[Queensland]], Australia.<ref>{{AIATSIS|G24|Mayi-Kutana}}</ref>

[[Gavan Breen]] (1981) thought that the [[Marrago]] might have been a sub-group of the Mayi Kutuna people; [[Paul Memmott]] (1994) lists the '''Marrago language''' separately but gives no further detail. Their status is unconfirmed by the [[AIATSIS]] collection.<ref>{{AIATSIS|G45|Marrago}}</ref>

